1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,168
JAKE:

2
00:00:02,168 --> 00:00:06,121
Now that we've done all the research into bike-sharing schemes in cities around the

3
00:00:06,121 --> 00:00:11,500
world, we need to think about how we're going to organise our report.

4
00:00:11,500 --> 00:00:11,581
AMY:

5
00:00:11,581 --> 00:00:11,722
Right.

6
00:00:11,722 --> 00:00:15,100
I think we should start by talking about the benefits.

7
00:00:15,100 --> 00:00:19,594
I mean it's great that so many cities have introduced these schemes where anyone

8
00:00:19,594 --> 00:00:22,803
can pick up a bike from dozens of different locations

9
00:00:22,803 --> 00:00:25,050
and hire it for a few hours.

10
00:00:25,050 --> 00:00:28,030
It makes riding a bike very convenient for people.

11
00:00:28,030 --> 00:00:28,080
JAKE:

12
00:00:28,080 --> 00:00:28,666
Yes,

13
00:00:28,666 --> 00:00:33,116
but the costs can add up and that puts people on low incomes off

14
00:00:33,116 --> 00:00:34,070
in some places.

15
00:00:34,070 --> 00:00:34,120
AMY:

16
00:00:34,120 --> 00:00:35,567
I suppose so,

17
00:00:35,567 --> 00:00:40,806
but if it means more people in general are cycling rather than driving, then

18
00:00:40,806 --> 00:00:46,046
because they're increasing the amount of physical activity they do, it's good for their

19
00:00:46,046 --> 00:00:46,420
health.

20
00:00:46,420 --> 00:00:46,470
JAKE:

21
00:00:46,470 --> 00:00:47,270
OK.

22
00:00:47,270 --> 00:00:49,820
But isn't that of less importance?

23
00:00:49,820 --> 00:00:55,295
I mean, doesn't the impact of reduced emissions on air pollution have a more

24
00:00:55,295 --> 00:00:57,250
significant effect on people's health?

25
00:00:57,250 --> 00:00:57,649
AMY:

26
00:00:57,649 --> 00:01:02,440
Certainly, in some cities bike-sharing has made a big contribution to that.

27
00:01:02,440 --> 00:01:06,480
And also helped to cut the number of cars on the road significantly.

28
00:01:06,480 --> 00:01:06,720
JAKE:

29
00:01:06,720 --> 00:01:07,920
Which is the main point.

30
00:01:07,920 --> 00:01:08,271
AMY:

31
00:01:08,271 --> 00:01:08,622
Exactly.

32
00:01:08,622 --> 00:01:12,837
But I'd say it's had less of an impact on noise pollution

33
00:01:12,837 --> 00:01:16,350
because there are still loads of buses and lorries around.

34
00:01:16,350 --> 00:01:16,820
JAKE:

35
00:01:16,820 --> 00:01:17,290
Right.

36
00:01:17,290 --> 00:01:17,632
AMY:

37
00:01:17,632 --> 00:01:21,050
Shall we quickly discuss the recommendations we're going to make?

38
00:01:21,050 --> 00:01:21,351
JAKE:

39
00:01:21,351 --> 00:01:23,760
In order to ensure bike-sharing schemes are successful?

40
00:01:23,760 --> 00:01:24,195
AMY:

41
00:01:24,195 --> 00:01:24,630
Yes.

42
00:01:24,630 --> 00:01:25,028
JAKE:

43
00:01:25,028 --> 00:01:25,426
OK.

44
00:01:25,426 --> 00:01:25,824
Well,

45
00:01:25,824 --> 00:01:31,395
while I think it's nice to have really state-of-the art bikes with things like

46
00:01:31,395 --> 00:01:34,180
GPS, I wouldn't say they're absolutely necessary.

47
00:01:34,180 --> 00:01:34,616
AMY:

48
00:01:34,616 --> 00:01:39,847
But some technical things are really important – like a fully functional app –

49
00:01:39,847 --> 00:01:43,770
so people can make payments and book bikes easily.

50
00:01:43,770 --> 00:01:47,440
Places which haven't invested in that have really struggled.

51
00:01:47,440 --> 00:01:47,824
JAKE:

52
00:01:47,824 --> 00:01:48,591
Good point ...

53
00:01:48,591 --> 00:01:53,194
Some people say there shouldn't be competing companies offering separate bike-sharing schemes,

54
00:01:53,194 --> 00:01:58,565
but in some really big cities, competition's beneficial and anyway one company might not

55
00:01:58,565 --> 00:02:01,250
be able to manage the whole thing.

56
00:02:01,250 --> 00:02:01,686
AMY:

57
00:02:01,686 --> 00:02:02,123
Right.

58
00:02:02,123 --> 00:02:06,050
Deciding how much to invest is a big question.

59
00:02:06,050 --> 00:02:11,811
Cities which have opened loads of new bike lanes at the same time as

60
00:02:11,811 --> 00:02:17,161
introducing bike-sharing schemes have generally been more successful – but there are examples of

61
00:02:17,161 --> 00:02:19,630
successful schemes where this hasn't happened …

62
00:02:19,630 --> 00:02:23,310
What does matter though – is having a big publicity campaign.

63
00:02:23,310 --> 00:02:23,626
JAKE:

64
00:02:23,626 --> 00:02:23,941
Definitely.

65
00:02:23,941 --> 00:02:28,358
If people don't know how to use the scheme or don't understand its benefits,

66
00:02:28,358 --> 00:02:29,620
they won't use it.

67
00:02:29,620 --> 00:02:37,210
People need a lot of persuasion to stop using their cars.

68
00:02:37,210 --> 00:02:37,565
AMY:

69
00:02:37,565 --> 00:02:40,050
Shall we look at some examples now?

70
00:02:40,050 --> 00:02:43,420
And say what we think is good or bad about them.

71
00:02:43,420 --> 00:02:43,678
JAKE:

72
00:02:43,678 --> 00:02:47,284
I suppose we should start with Amsterdam as this was one of the first

73
00:02:47,284 --> 00:02:48,830
cities to have a bike-sharing scheme.

74
00:02:48,830 --> 00:02:49,245
AMY:

75
00:02:49,245 --> 00:02:49,661
Yes.

76
00:02:49,661 --> 00:02:53,400
There was already a strong culture of cycling here.

77
00:02:53,400 --> 00:02:57,425
In a way it's strange that there was such a demand for bike-sharing

78
00:02:57,425 --> 00:03:01,140
because you'd have thought most people would have used their own bikes.

79
00:03:01,140 --> 00:03:01,391
JAKE:

80
00:03:01,391 --> 00:03:03,400
And yet it's one of the best-used schemes ...

81
00:03:03,400 --> 00:03:07,130
Dublin's an interesting example of a success story.

82
00:03:07,130 --> 00:03:07,468
AMY:

83
00:03:07,468 --> 00:03:10,850
It must be because the public transport system's quite limited.

84
00:03:10,850 --> 00:03:11,152
JAKE:

85
00:03:11,152 --> 00:03:12,662
Not really – there's no underground,

86
00:03:12,662 --> 00:03:15,380
but there are trams and a good bus network.

87
00:03:15,380 --> 00:03:18,580
I'd say price has a lot to do with it.

88
00:03:18,580 --> 00:03:21,380
It's one of the cheapest schemes in Europe to join.

89
00:03:21,380 --> 00:03:21,781
AMY:

90
00:03:21,781 --> 00:03:27,390
But the buses are really slow – anyway the weather certainly can't be a factor!

91
00:03:27,390 --> 00:03:27,824
JAKE:

92
00:03:27,824 --> 00:03:29,126
No – definitely not.

93
00:03:29,126 --> 00:03:31,730
The London scheme's been quite successful.

94
00:03:31,730 --> 00:03:32,016
AMY:

95
00:03:32,016 --> 00:03:34,880
Yes – it's been a really good thing for the city.

96
00:03:34,880 --> 00:03:39,086
The bikes are popular and the whole system is well maintained

97
00:03:39,086 --> 00:03:41,380
but it isn't expanding quickly enough.

98
00:03:41,380 --> 00:03:41,722
JAKE:

99
00:03:41,722 --> 00:03:45,832
Basically, not enough's been spent on increasing the number of cycle lanes.

100
00:03:45,832 --> 00:03:46,860
Hopefully that'll change.

101
00:03:46,860 --> 00:03:47,506
AMY:

102
00:03:47,506 --> 00:03:48,151
Yes.

103
00:03:48,151 --> 00:03:51,380
Now what about outside Europe?

104
00:03:51,380 --> 00:03:51,711
JAKE:

105
00:03:51,711 --> 00:03:55,350
Well bike-sharing schemes have taken off in places like Buenos Aires.

106
00:03:55,350 --> 00:03:55,646
AMY:

107
00:03:55,646 --> 00:03:55,942
Mmm.

108
00:03:55,942 --> 00:04:00,086
They built a huge network of cycle lanes to support the introduction of the

109
00:04:00,086 --> 00:04:01,270
scheme there, didn't they?

110
00:04:01,270 --> 00:04:06,070
It attracted huge numbers of cyclists where previously there were hardly any.

111
00:04:06,070 --> 00:04:06,372
JAKE:

112
00:04:06,372 --> 00:04:07,880
An example of good planning.

113
00:04:07,880 --> 00:04:08,251
AMY:

114
00:04:08,251 --> 00:04:08,621
Absolutely.

115
00:04:08,621 --> 00:04:13,440
New York is a good example of how not to introduce a scheme.

116
00:04:13,440 --> 00:04:18,331
When they launched it, it was more than ten times the price of most

117
00:04:18,331 --> 00:04:19,030
other schemes.

118
00:04:19,030 --> 00:04:19,281
JAKE:

119
00:04:19,281 --> 00:04:21,290
More than it costs to take a taxi.

120
00:04:21,290 --> 00:04:21,768
Crazy.

121
00:04:21,768 --> 00:04:26,070
I think the organisers lacked vision and ambition there.

122
00:04:26,070 --> 00:04:26,403
AMY:

123
00:04:26,403 --> 00:04:27,735
I think so too.

124
00:04:27,735 --> 00:04:30,400
Sydney would be a good example to use.

125
00:04:30,400 --> 00:04:33,450
I would have expected it to have grown pretty quickly here.

126
00:04:33,450 --> 00:04:33,776
JAKE:

127
00:04:33,776 --> 00:04:34,102
Yes.

128
00:04:34,102 --> 00:04:38,663
I can't quite work out why it hasn't been an instant success like some

129
00:04:38,663 --> 00:04:39,640
of the others.

130
00:04:39,640 --> 00:04:41,230
It's a shame really.

131
00:04:41,230 --> 00:04:41,760
AMY:

132
00:04:41,760 --> 00:04:42,820
I know.

133
00:04:42,820 --> 00:04:46,000
OK so now we've thought about ...
